Lumia 1520 (RM-940) -- WCD9320 codec core lifecycle test
Kernel 6.16.12 #120-postmarketos-qcom-msm8974, boot-1520-wcd9320-core.img (r119)
2026-07-30

Modules installed into /lib/modules and depmod run, then:

  # modprobe wcd9320
  MODPROBE_OK
  wcd9320                12288  0
  regmap_slimbus         12288  1 wcd9320

modules.dep carries the dependency, so no hand-ordered insmod is needed:
  kernel/drivers/slimbus/wcd9320.ko: kernel/drivers/base/regmap/regmap-slimbus.ko

Initial enumeration:
  device UP (#1), logical address 0xcb
  id_major 0x0102 id_minor 0x0001 version 0x00 (raw 01 00 02 01 00)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Lifecycle test: 10 cycles
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  # echo 10 > /sys/bus/slimbus/devices/217:a0:1:0/lifecycle_test

Each cycle: assert reset, slim_report_absent(), verify the cached logical
address was invalidated, cycle the supplies, release reset, force a fresh
address query, rebuild regmap on UP, re-read the identity.

  lifecycle test: 10/10 cycles OK (up 11, down 10)

  cycles OK             : 10
  laddr NOT invalidated : 0
  re-enumeration failed : 0
  regmap not rebuilt    : 0
  identity read failed  : 0
  not-Taiko             : 0
  map not responding    : 0
  ADSP after            : running

The logical address came back as 0xcb on every cycle. The driver tolerates
it changing; on this hardware it happened not to.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEFECT FOUND BY THIS TEST -- regmap leak (not yet fixed)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  debugfs: Directory '217:a0:1:0' with parent 'regmap' already present!

repeated once per cycle from cycle 7 onward.

The driver calls devm_regmap_init_slimbus() on every device_status(UP) and
only sets wcd->regmap = NULL on DOWN. Setting a pointer to NULL does not
free a devres allocation, so each cycle leaks a regmap; eleven now exist and
each tries to create the same debugfs directory. Only the first succeeds.

The design assumption behind that was wrong. regmap does not ride on the
logical address -- it rides on the slim_device, which persists across
absence. slim_read() reads sdev->laddr at call time, so one regmap stays
valid through re-enumeration and only the reads fail while the device is
down.

Correct shape: create the regmap once in probe and keep it, rather than
rebuilding it per UP. Reads attempted while the device is down will fail on
their own.

Functionally the test passed -- every read returned correct data -- but the
resource handling is wrong and is recorded here as an open defect.
